  :adr/body
  "# ADR-2607196000: Kotoba PostgreSQL SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS channel binding

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-19

## Decision

When a PostgreSQL TLS server advertises `SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS` followed by
`SCRAM-SHA-256`, the Kotoba provider selects PLUS. The `.kotoba` component
constructs the `p=tls-server-end-point,,` GS2 header, channel-binding `c=`
attribute, auth message, proof request and final response. A base-only server
continues to use `n,,` and `c=biws`.

The host exposes one bounded `tls_server_end_point(handle,out,cap)` operation.
It accepts only a live affine TLS handle and returns the RFC 5929 digest of the
authenticated peer certificate. For MD5/SHA-1 certificate signatures it uses
SHA-256; otherwise it follows the SHA-2 signature family. It does not expose a
socket, certificate private key, root secret, trust-store mutation, or TLS
verification disable switch. PostgreSQL itself verifies that the supplied
binding matches the active TLS channel.

## Evidence and maturity boundary

The released PostgreSQL runner now records client writes, asserts the selected
mechanism is exactly `SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS`, completes authentication and
`select 1`, checks affine handle cleanup, then stops and deletes the ephemeral
cluster. Unit tests retain the base-only fallback and verify exact PLUS GS2 and
base64 channel-binding construction.

This closes the channel-binding gap recorded by ADR-2607195900. The
supported-version matrix, external CA/revocation/rotation, client
certificates, credential rotation, cancellation, transactions, prepared
statements, pooling and long-duration soak remain open; therefore this is not
yet a production-qualified PostgreSQL client."}]
